he remaining points is non‑sunny; a sunny line can contain at most one of them.  \n  Since there are three such points, two sunny lines cannot cover them all.\n\n* **Case \\(c=2\\).**  \n  Points on \\(L_{0}\\): \\(C,F\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{A(1,1),B(1,2),D(1,3),E(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  - \\(A,B,D\\) are vertical (\\(x=1\\)).  \n  - \\(A,E\\) are horizontal (\\(y=1\\)).  \n  - \\(B,E\\) satisfy \\(x+y=4\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n  Again every pair lies on a non‑sunny line, so the two sunny lines can cover at most two of the four points – impossible.\n\n* **Case \\(c=3\\).**  \n  Points on \\(L_{0}\\): \\(E\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{A,B,C,D,F\\}\\).  \n  - \\(A,B,D\\) vertical;  \n  - \\(A,C\\) horizontal;  \n  - \\(B,F\\) satisfy \\(x+y=4\\).  \n  As before, any two are collinear on a non‑sunny line, leaving at least five points to be covered by only two sunny lines – impossible.\n\nHence a vertical non‑sunny line cannot be the unique non‑sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  \\(L_{0}\\) is horizontal, \\(L_{0}:y=c\\).\n\nBy symmetry with the vertical case (interchanging the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)), the admissible constants are \\(c=1,2,3\\).  \nThe same analysis shows that the set of points not on \\(L_{0}\\) always contains a pair whose connecting line is vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most one of those points, while there are at least three such points.  \nThus a horizontal \\(L_{0}\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  \\(L_{0}\\) has slope \\(-1\\), \\(L_{0}:x+y=c\\).\n\nThe sums of coordinates of points in \\(\\mathcal P\\) are \\(2,3,4\\); therefore \\(c\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\).\n\n* **Case \\(c=2\\).**  \n  Points on \\(L_{0}\\): \\(A\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{B(1,2),C(2,1),D(1,3),E(3,1),F(2,2)\\}\\).  \n  - \\(B\\) and \\(D\\) vertical;  \n  - \\(C\\) and \\(E\\) horizont