outside** all non\u2011sunny lines.\nSince each sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}2\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(S_n\\) (the longest possible segment of slope different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) inside the triangle has that length), the total number of\npoints that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}2\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 1.3  Combined bound  \n\nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}2\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) the right\u2013hand side of (4) is **strictly smaller**\nthan the left\u2013hand side.  Indeed, write \\(n=2m\\) or \\(n=2m+1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\):* \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}2\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\).  \nThen (4) becomes  \n\n\\[\nm(2m+1)\\le (2m-k)2m+km\n      =2m^2+km-mk .\n\\]\n\nCancelling \\(2m^2\\) gives \\(m\\le km-mk=0\\), a contradiction for any\n\\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\):* \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}2\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\).  \nInequality (4) reads  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(m+1)\\le (2m+1-k)(2m+1)+(m+1)k .\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2013hand side simplifies to \\((2m+1)(m+1)-k\\), which is smaller than\nthe left\u2013hand side because \\(k\\ge2\\).  Hence (4) cannot hold.\n\nThus no configuration with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) can cover all points of\n\\(S_n\\).  Consequently the only possible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,n\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines realising each of the three values in (5).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n\\(L_b\\).  All points are covered and exactly \\(0\\) lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\ntogether with the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\;x-y=0\\quad\\text{(i.e. }y=x\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThe horizontal lines cover every point with \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \nThe remaining points are those with \\(b=n\\) or with \\(a=b\\) and\n\\(b\\le n\\); all of them satisfy \\(x-y=0\\).  Hence the whole set \\(S_n\\) is\ncovered.  The line \\(y=x\\) has slope \\(+1\\), which is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny, while the other \\(n-1\\) lines are\nnon\u2011sunny.  Thus exactly one sunny line occurs.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3  \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines of slope \\(+1\\)\n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x-y=c\\qquad\\text{for }c=-(n-1),-(n-3),\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe constants form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(2\\); there are\nexactly \\(n\\) of them.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) the integer \\(c=a-b\\) lies in\nthe interval \\([-(n-1),\\,n-1]\\) and has the same parity as \\(a+b\\).  Because\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the parity forces \\(c\\) to be one of the listed constants,\nso \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the corresponding line \\(L_c\\).  Consequently the\n\\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  Their slope is \\(+1\\), which is\nneither \\(0\\), nor \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\); hence every line is sunny.\nThus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n* no configuration with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) sunny lines can cover all required\n  points, and\n* configurations exist for \\(k=0\\), for \\(k=1\\) and for \\(k=n\\).\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,n\\}} .\n\\]"

