n   Hence we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) the right–hand side of (2) is **strictly smaller** than the left–hand side; a direct verification separates the two parity cases:\n\n   *If \\(n=2m\\) (even):* \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\).  \n   Inequality (2) becomes \\(m(2m+1)\\le (2m-k)2m+km\\), i.e. \\(m\\le 0\\), impossible for \\(m\\ge1\\).\n\n   *If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd):* \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\).  \n   (2) rewrites as \\((2m+1)(m+1)\\le (2m+1-k)(2m+1)+(m+1)k\\), which simplifies to \\((2m+1)(m+1)\\le (2m+1)(m+1)-k\\), again impossible because \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n   Consequently **no** configuration exists with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), hence lies on \\(L_b\\).  \n   All lines are horizontal, therefore \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n   \\]\n\n   (all non‑sunny) together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y-2x+ (n+1)=0\\qquad\\text{or equivalently }y=2x-(n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S\\) passes through the single point \\((n,1)\\) (the only point of \\(S_n\\) with \\(x=n\\)).  \n   For \\(n\\ge3\\) its slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(S\\) is sunny.  \n   The vertical lines cover every point with \\(x\\le n-1\\); the remaining point \\((n,1)\\) is on \\(S\\).  \n   Hence all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n6. **When can all \\(n\\) lines be sunny?**  \n   From (1) each sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing with \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality holds **only for \\(n=3\\)** (for \\(n\\ge4\\) the left‑hand side is smaller by at least \\(n/2\\)).  \n   Therefore \\(k=n\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\).\n\n7. **Explicit construction for \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).**  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y-x=0 \\quad\\text{(slope }1),\\\\\n   L_2 &: 2y+x-5=0 \\quad\\text{(slope }-2),\\\\\n   L_3 &: 2y- x-1=0 \\quad\\text{(slope } \\tfrac12)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Their integer points inside the triangle \\(\\{a+b\\le4\\}\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;(1,1),(2,2),\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;(1,3),(3,1),\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;(1,2),(2,1),\n   \\]\n\n   which together constitute the whole set \\(S_3\\).  \n   Hence for \\(n=3\\) we indeed have a configuration with \\(k=3\\).\n\n8. **Collecting the possibilities.**  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\).  \n   * By step 3 no configuration exists with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   * By step 6 and the explicit example of step 7, \\(k=n\\) is possible exactly when \\(n=3\\).\n\n   Consequently the complete list of admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n   \\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge4 .\n   \\end{cases}}\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the\n\\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A bound for a sunny line\n\nWrite a line in the form  \n\n\\[\nax+by=c,\\qquad a,b,c\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(a,b)=1 .\n\\]\n\nIf the line is sunny then \\(a\\neq0,\\;b\\neq0\\) and \\(|a|\\neq|b|\\); otherwise the\nline would be horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\).\n\nTwo consecutive lattice points on the line differ by  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\frac{b}{g},-\\frac{a}{g}\\Bigr),\\qquad g=\\gcd(a,b)=1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the change of the sum \\(x+y\\) between consecutive lattice points equals  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta (x+y)=\\frac{b-a}{1}=b-a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|a|\\neq|b|\\) and both are non‑zero, \\(|b-a|\\ge2\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\Delta (x+y)|\\ge2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInside the triangle \\(\\{a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) the quantity \\(x+y\\) ranges from\n\\(2\\) up to \\(n+1\\), a total length of \\(n-1\\).  By (2) at most one point of\n\\(S_n\\) can appear every two successive integer values of \\(x+y\\); consequently a\nsunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (\\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)).\nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them meets the triangle in\nat most \\(n\\) lattice points (a horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line can\ncontain at most one point for each possible value of the remaining coordinate,\nhence at most \\(n\\) points).  Thus the non‑sunny lines together cover at most\n\\((n-k)n\\) points.\n\nBy (3) each sunny line covers at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most\n\\(k\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWe show that (4) is never satisfied when \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) (even):* \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\).  \n(4) becomes  \n\n\\[\nm(2m+1)\\le (2m-k)2m+km =2m^{2}+km-mk .\n\\]\n\nCancelling \\(2m^{2}\\) yields \\(m\\le0\\), impossible for \\(m\\ge1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd):* \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\).  \n(4) rewrites as  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(m+1)\\le (2m+1-k)(2m+1)+(m+1)k .\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side simplifies to \\((2m+1)(m+1)-k\\), which is strictly smaller\nthan the left‑hand side because \\(k\\ge2\\).  Hence (4) cannot hold.\n\nThus **no** configuration exists with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n##### (a)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so it lies on \\(L_b\\).\nAll lines are non‑sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n##### (b)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x-(n+1).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S\\) has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it passes through the unique\npoint of \\(S_n\\) with \\(x=n\\), namely \\((n,1)\\).  \nAll points with \\(x\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical lines, and \\((n,1)\\) is\ncovered by \\(S\\).  Hence the whole set \\(S_n\\) is covered and exactly one line\nis sunny.\n\n##### (c)  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFrom (3) a sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  Consequently \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints.  Comparing with \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThis inequality holds only for \\(n=3\\); for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the left‑hand side is\nsmaller by at least \\(n/2\\).  Hence a configuration with all lines sunny is\npossible **only when \\(n=3\\)**.\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) an explicit family of three sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-x=0 \\quad(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\nL_2 &: 2y+x-5=0 \\quad(\\text{slope }-2),\\\\\nL_3 &: 2y-x-1=0 \\quad(\\text{slope }\\tfrac12).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir integer points inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le4\\) are\n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;(1,1),(2,2),\\qquad\nL_2:\\;(1,3),(3,1),\\qquad\nL_3:\\;(1,2),(2,1),\n\\]\n\nwhich together constitute the whole set \\(S_3\\).  All three lines are sunny,\nso \\(k=3\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final classification\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\).\n* By the impossibility argument of Section 2, no configuration exists with\n  \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n* By the counting argument of Section 3(c) and the explicit example for\n  \\(n=3\\), the case \\(k=n\\) is possible exactly when \\(n=3\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
