efore we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWe show that (4) is never satisfied when \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) (even):* \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\).  \n(4) becomes  \n\n\\[\nm(2m+1)\\le (2m-k)2m+km =2m^{2}+km-mk .\n\\]\n\nCancelling \\(2m^{2}\\) yields \\(m\\le0\\), impossible for \\(m\\ge1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd):* \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\).  \n(4) rewrites as  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(m+1)\\le (2m+1-k)(2m+1)+(m+1)k .\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side simplifies to \\((2m+1)(m+1)-k\\), which is strictly smaller\nthan the left‑hand side because \\(k\\ge2\\).  Hence (4) cannot hold.\n\nThus **no** configuration exists with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n##### (a)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so it lies on \\(L_b\\).\nAll lines are non‑sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n##### (b)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x-(n+1).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S\\) has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it passes through the unique\npoint of \\(S_n\\) with \\(x=n\\), namely \\((n,1)\\).  \nAll points with \\(x\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical lines, and \\((n,1)\\) is\ncovered by \\(S\\).  Hence the whole set \\(S_n\\) is covered and exactly one line\nis sunny.\n\n##### (c)  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFrom (3) a sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  Consequently \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints.  Comparing with \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) we obtain  \n\n