            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (size of a sunny line).**  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n   \\[\n   |L\\cap S_n|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  Write the line as \\(ax+by=c\\) with integers \\(a,b,c\\) and\n   \\(\\gcd(a,b)=1\\).  \n   Sunny means \\(a\\neq0,\\;b\\neq0\\) and \\(|a|\\neq|b|\\).  \n   The set of integer points on the line is a one‑dimensional lattice with\n   step vector \\((b,-a)\\); consecutive lattice points differ by this vector.\n   Hence the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by \\(\\Delta:=b-a\\) when we move from one lattice\n   point to the next.  Because \\(|a|\\neq|b|\\) we have \\(|\\Delta|\\ge 1\\).\n\n   Inside the triangle \\(\\{a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) the quantity \\(x+y\\) ranges\n   over the interval \\([2,n+1]\\), whose length is \\(n-1\\).  Starting from the\n   smallest possible value of \\(x+y\\) on the line, each step increases (or\n   decreases) the sum by \\(|\\Delta|\\).  Consequently the number of lattice\n   points of the line that can lie in the triangle is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{1}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   This proves (1). ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (size of a non‑sunny line).**  \n   Any non‑sunny line meets the triangle in at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  A horizontal line \\(y=t\\) contains the points \\((a,t)\\) with\n   \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\); the number of such points is \\(n+1-t\\le n\\).  \n   The same bound holds for a vertical line and for a line \\(x+y=c\\). ∎\n\n4. **Counting argument.**  \n   Suppose we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of\n   \\(S_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   By Lemma 2 the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\)\n   points; by Lemma 1 the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most\n   \\(k\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   We analyse (2).\n\n   *If \\(n=2m\\) is even*, then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and\n   (2) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   m(2m+1)\\le (2m-k)2m+km\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   m\\le k .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd*, then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and\n   (2) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   (2m+1)(m+1)\\le (2m+1-k)(2m+1)+(m+1)k\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   m+1\\le k .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\begin{cases}\n   m, & n=2m,\\\\[1mm]\n   m+1, & n=2m+1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because we always have \\(k\\le n\\), (5) forces\n\n   * either \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\);\n   * or \\(k=n\\) **and** the equality in (5) holds, i.e. \\(k=n=m\\) (even case) or\n     \\(k=n=2m+1\\) with \\(m+1=n\\), which is possible only for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   Consequently, for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the only possible values are \\(k=0\\) and\n   \\(k=1\\); for \\(n=3\\) the possibilities are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n5. **Explicit constructions.**\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   Every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the\n   line \\(y=b\\).  All lines are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\)\n   (all non‑sunny) and add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=2x-(n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence it is sunny, and it\n   passes through the unique point of \\(S_n\\) with \\(x=n\\), namely \\((n,1)\\).\n   The vertical lines cover all points with \\(x\\le n-1\\); together the \\(n\\) lines\n   cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\) for \\(n=3\\).*  The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: y-x=0 \\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\n   \\ell_2 &: 2y+x-5=0 \\qquad(\\text{slope }-2),\\\\\n   \\ell_3 &: 2y-x-1=0 \\qquad(\\text{slope }\\tfrac12)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis\n   or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Their integer points inside the triangle\n   \\(a+b\\le4\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;(1,1),(2,2),\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;(1,3),(3,1),\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\;(1,2),(2,1),\n   \\]\n\n   which together constitute the whole set \\(S_3\\).  Hence \\(k=3\\) is\n   attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\).*  A direct inspection of the six\n   points of \\(S_3\\) shows that any line containing two of them is either\n   horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); therefore 