; the number is \\(n+1-t\\le n\\).  The same bound holds for a\nvertical line and for a line \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nWrite a sunny line in reduced integer form \\(ax+by=c\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(a,b)=1\\), \\(a\\neq0\\), \\(b\\neq0\\) and \\(|a|\\neq|b|\\).\nThe set of integer points on the line is a one‑dimensional lattice generated by\n\\((b,-a)\\).  Moving from one integer point to the next changes the sum\n\\(x+y\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=b-a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|a|\\neq|b|\\) we have \\(|\\Delta|\\ge2\\).  Inside the triangle\n\\(\\{x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) the quantity \\(x+y\\) ranges from \\(2\\) to\n\\(n+1\\), an interval of length \\(n-1\\).  Hence the number of integer points of\nthe line that can lie in the triangle is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\ns_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (A lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nM_t=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=t\\},\\qquad |M_t|=t-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf a line is not of the form \\(x+y=t\\) then it contains **at most one** point of\neach set \\(M_t\\) (the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by a non‑zero amount when we move\nalong the line).  Consequently, after the \\(k\\) sunny lines have been placed,\nat least \\(\\max\\{0,(t-1)-k\\}\\) points of \\(M_t\\) still have to be covered by\nnon‑sunny lines, and a non‑sunny line can cover at most one of them unless it is\nthe diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\).  Therefore the total number of non‑sunny lines\nrequired is at least  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}\\max\\{0,(t-1)-k\\}\n   =\\sum_{t=k+2}^{n+1}(t-1-k)\n   =\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Main counting inequality\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and exactly\n\\(k\\) of them are sunny.  The \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can together contain at\nmost \\((n-k)n\\) points (Lemma 1), while the \\(k\\) sunny lines can together\ncontain at most \\(k\\,s_n\\) points.  Even if the two families are disjoint they\nmust cover \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points, so  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+k\\,s_n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequences of Lemma 2\n\nFrom (2) we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n-k\\ge2\\) the right‑hand side exceeds the left‑hand side, a contradiction.\nHence  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\le1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge n-1 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Elimination of the possibilities \\(k=n\\) (even \\(n\\)) and \\(k=n-1\\)\n\n*Even \\(n\\).*  \nLet \\(n=2m\\).  Then \\(s_n=m\\).  If \\(k=n\\) all lines are sunny, the total\nnumber of points they can cover is at most \\(n\\,s_n=2m\\cdot m=2m^{2}\\), whereas\n\\(|S_n|=m(2m+1)=2m^{2}+m\\).  Thus \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\).\n\n*The case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nHere exactly one line is non‑sunny.  Using Lemma 1, the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines\ncover at most \\((n-1)s_n\\) points and the remaining line contributes at most\n\\(n\\) points; the total is at most \\((n-1)s_n+n\\).  A direct computation shows\n\n\\[\n(n-1)s_n+n\n   =\\begin{cases}\n      (2m-1)m+2m = 2m^{2}+m < m(2m+1) & (n=2m\\ge4),\\\\[2mm]\n      (2m)(m+1)+ (2m+1) = 2m^{2}+3m+1 < (2m+1)(m+1) & (n=2m+1\\ge5),\n    \\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nwhich is strictly smaller than \\(|S_n|\\).  Hence \\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur for any\n\\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Determination of the admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(k\\ge n-1\\); the two possibilities are \\(k=n-1\\) and\n\\(k=n\\).  Both have been shown impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere \\(|S_3|=6\\) and \\(s_3=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{4}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=2\\).\n\n*The bound (3) gives \\(k\\ge2\\).  The inequality (1) with \\(n=3,\\;s_3=2\\) reads\n\\(3\\cdot(3-k)+2k\\ge6\\), i.e. \\(k\\ge2\\).  Thus only \\(k=2\\) or \\(k=3\\) are\npossible besides the trivial constructions with \\(k=0,1\\).*\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \nAny line that contains two points of \\(S_3\\) has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or\n\\(-1\\) (a short inspection of the six points shows this).  Hence at most one\nof the three lines can be sunny, so \\(k=2\\) cannot be realised.\n\n*Realisation of \\(k=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:y-x=0,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:2y+x-5=0,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:2y-x-1=0\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the forbidden directions.\nTheir integer points inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le4\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:(1,1),(2,2),\\qquad\n\\ell_2:(1,3),(3,1),\\qquad\n\\ell_3:(1,2),(2,1),\n\\]\n\nwhich together form the whole set \\(S_3\\).  Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Explicit constructions for the admissible values\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all \\(n\\) lines non‑sunny): the horizontal lines\n  \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line): the vertical lines\n  \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line\n  \\(y=2x-(n+1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3\\): the three sunny lines displayed above.\n\nAll these families consist of distinct lines and cover every point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
