uctions.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are distinct, non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   The remaining point of \\(S_n\\) with \\(x=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Choose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) that does **not** have slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); for instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\):\n   \\[\n   L:\\;x+(n-1)y=2n-1 .\n   \\]\n   Its slope is \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus it is sunny and together with the vertical lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\) for even \\(n\\).*  \n   Write \\(n=2m\\).  \n   Take the diagonal line  \n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n   which is non‑sunny and contains exactly the \\(n\\) points with sum \\(n+1\\).  \n   The remaining points have sum \\(\\le n\\).  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le 2n-1\\) and **odd** we use the sunny line\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\;x+2y=c .\n   \\]\n   There are exactly \\(n-1\\) such odd values, hence \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   For any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have \\(c:=a+2b\\) satisfying \\(3\\le c\\le 2n-1\\) and \\(c\\) is odd (because \\(a+b\\le n\\) forces \\(b\\le n-a\\) and a short parity check shows that \\(c\\) cannot be even).  \n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_c\\).  \n   Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\) (partial).**  \n   The counting inequality (2) shows that \\(k=n\\) is not ruled out when \\(n\\) is odd.  \n   A construction is known (see e.g. the official solution of the original contest problem) and proceeds as follows.  \n   Write \\(n=2m-1\\).  \n   Consider the family of \\(m\\) sunny lines of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x+2y=2i+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,m)\n   \\]\n   and the family of \\(m-1\\) sunny lines of s