al of length at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n> Hence **any sunny line contains at most** \\(s_n:=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe description for \\(|\\Delta|\\ge2\\) is correct: the arithmetic progression of possible values of \\(x+y\\) has step at least\u202f2, so at most \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor+1\\) terms lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\).  \n\nFor \\(|\\Delta|=1\\) the argument is **incomplete**. The line has direction vector \\((t+1,-t)\\) (or the symmetric one). While it is true that moving one step changes \\(x+y\\) by\u202f1, the claim that \u201cpositivity forces the parameter \u2026 to stay in an interval of length at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\)\u201d is not justified. One must show that the number of integer points with both coordinates positive and with \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) cannot exceed \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\). This requires a careful analysis of the inequalities\n\\[\nx_0 + (t+1)u >0,\\qquad y_0 - t u >0,\\qquad x_0+y_0 + u \\le n+1,\n\\]\nand a bound on the admissible integer values of \\(u\\). The solution merely states the bound without proof. Consequently this step contains a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Derivation of the counting inequality  \n\n> \u201cLet exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines be sunny.  \n> The \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines together contain at most \\((n-k)n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); the \\(k\\) sunny lines together contain at most \\(k\\,s_n\\) points.  \n> Since every point of \\(S_n\\) must belong to at least one line, we obtain (1) and consequently (2).\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nAssuming Lemma\u202f1 is correct, the inequality\n\\[\n(n-k)n + k s_n \\ge |S_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\]\nfollows directly from the pigeon\u2011hole principle. Solving it yields\n\\[\nk\\le\\begin{cases}\nn-1 & (n\\text{ even}),\\\\[2mm]\nn   & (n\\text{ odd}),\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwhich is algebraically correct. No error is present here.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Construction for \\(k=0\\)  \n\n> \u201cThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are distinct, non\u2011sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge1\\)). Hence each point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\). The lines are non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis). The construction is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Construction for \\(k=1\\)  \n\n> \u201cUse the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n> The remaining point of \\(S_n\\) with \\(x=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\).  \n> Choose any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) that does **not** have slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); for instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\): \\(x+(n-1)y=2n-1\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe vertical lines cover all points with \\(x\\le n-1\\). The only point with \\(x=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\). The line \\(x+(n-1)y=2n-1\\) has slope \\(-1/(n-1)\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence it is sunny and covers the remaining point. The construction is valid.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even  \n\n> \u201cWrite \\(n=2m\\).  \n> Take the diagonal line \\(D:\\;x+y=n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n> For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le 2n-1\\) **and odd** we use the sunny line \\(L_c:\\;x+2y=c\\).  \n> \u2026 For any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have \\(c:=a+2b\\) \u2026 consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_c\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe claim that **every** point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) satisfies \\(c=a+2b\\) odd is false. Counterexample (with \\(n=4\\), i.e. \\(m=2\\)):\n\\[\n(a,b)=(2,1)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a+b=3\\le4,\\qquad c=2+2\\cdot1=4\\ (\\text{even}).\n\\]\nSince only lines with **odd** right\u2011hand side are used, the point \\((2,1)\\) is not covered. Similar counterexamples exist for all even \\(n\\ge4\\). Therefore