Analysis.**  \nThe argument presumes the existence of an extremal configuration with the maximal possible number of sunny lines (i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) for even \\(n\\) and \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)). As shown in items\u202f5 and\u202f6, the purported extremal configurations are **incorrect**. Without a valid extremal configuration the replacement process cannot be initiated, so the claim that all intermediate values of \\(k\\) are attainable is unsupported. This constitutes a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. The case \\(n=3\\)  \n\n> \u201cThe impossibility of \\(k=2\\) follows from a direct inspection: any line that contains two points of \\(S_3\\) must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore at most one of the three lines can be sunny.\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe statement \u201cany line that contains two points of \\(S_3\\) must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)\u201d is **false**. The line \\(y=x\\) (slope\u202f1) contains the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), both belonging to \\(S_3\\), and is sunny. Hence the reasoning is incorrect. Although the final conclusion that \\(k=2\\) is impossible may still be true (a separate exhaustive search confirms it), the proof given is invalid. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Inconsistent discussion of the odd\u2011\\(n\\) extremal case  \n\n> \u201cThe only missing piece is an explicit, fully verified construction for the case \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd \u2026\u201d  \n> *Later*: \u201cAltogether we have \\(m+(m-1)=2m-1=n\\) sunny lines. \u2026 Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered \u2026\u201d\n\n**Analysis.**  \nThe summary first admits that a rigorous construction for \\(k=n\\) (odd \\(n\\)) is missing, yet the body of the solution presents a construction that, as shown in item\u202f6, is **incorrect**. This inconsistency reflects a **Justification Gap** (the required construction is not supplied) and compounds the **Critical Error** identified in the construction itself.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution correctly derives the necessary inequality (2) and provides valid constructions for the trivial cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\). However, the core of the answer\u2014showing that the extremal numbers of sunny lines are attainable and that all intermediate values follow\u2014relies on **faulty constructions** (items\u202f5 and\u202f6) and an **invalid argument** (item\u202f7). Moreover, Lemma\u202f1 is not proved with sufficient rigor (item\u202f1), and the case \\(n=3\\) contains a false claim (item\u202f5). Because the existence of configurations for the maximal values of \\(k\\) is not established, the final classification of admissible \\(k\\) is unsupported. Consequently, the solution is **invalid**."

