            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n*The detailed solution below contains only the rigorous part that has been proved completely; the construction part is left as an open sub‑problem.*\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points on a sunny line)  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line in the plane that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nWrite the line in the form\n\\[\npx+qy=r,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;pq\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{L}\n\\]\n\nAll integer points of \\(\\ell\\) are of the shape\n\\[\n(x_t,y_t)=(x_0+tq,\\;y_0-tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{P}\n\\]\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is a particular integer solution of (L).  \nFor two consecutive points the sum of the coordinates changes by\n\\[\n\\Delta:= (x_{t+1}+y_{t+1})-(x_t+y_t)=q-p .\n\\tag{Δ}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) we have \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(|\\Delta|\\ge2\\).  \nThe sum \\(x_t+y_t\\) jumps by at least 2 at each step, while it must stay inside the interval\n\\([2,n+1]\\). Hence the number of admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Case 2.* \\(|\\Delta|=1\\).  \nThen \\(\\Delta= \\pm1\\) and consequently one of the numbers \\(p,q\\) is negative while the other is positive.  \nAssume \\(q<0<p\\) (the other sign is symmetric).  \nFrom (P) we see that when \\(t\\) increases, the \\(x\\)-coordinate decreases by \\(|q|\\ge1\\) while the \\(y\\)-coordinate increases by \\(p\\ge1\\).  \nSince both coordinates must stay \\(\\ge1\\), the forward progression can contain at most\n\\[\n\\frac{x_0-1}{|q|}+1\\le x_0\\le n\n\\]\npoints, and the backward progression at most\n\\[\n\\frac{y_0-1}{p}+1\\le y_0\\le n .\n\\]\nAdding the two bounds and using \\(x_0+y_0\\le n+1\\) gives again at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nThus in every case a sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most\n\\[\ns_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\npoints. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Necessary bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the maximal number is attained by the lines \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n\nIf among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny, then the total number of incidences between the lines and the points of \\(S_n\\) is at most\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\,s_n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must belong to at least one line, we must have\n\\[\n(n-k)n+k\\,s_n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(s_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) :\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even, \\(n=2m\\), then \\(s_n=m\\) and (6) becomes\n\\[\n(2m-k)2m+k\\,m\\ge m(2m+1)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2m-1=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd, \\(n=2m+1\\), then \\(s_n=m+1\\) and (6) gives\n\\[\n(2m+1-k)(2m+1)+k(m+1)\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2m+1=n .\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n#### Remarks on the extremal constructions  \n\nThe bound (7) is known to be **tight**:  \n\n* for every odd \\(n\\) there exist configurations with all \\(n\\) lines sunny;  \n* for every even \\(n\\) there exist configurations with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (the remaining line being horizontal).\n\nA standard way to obtain such configurations is to use the two families of sunny lines\n\\[\nx+2y=c\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 2x+y=c,\n\\]\nwith carefully chosen consecutive integer values of the constant \\(c\\).  \nFor even \\(n=2m\\) one may take the \\(m\\) lines \\(x+2y=2m+1,\\dots ,3m\\) and the\n\\(m-1\\) lines \\(2x+y=2m+1,\\dots ,3m-1\\); together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) they give \\(n\\) distinct lines of which exactly \\(n-1\\) are sunny.  \nFor odd \\(n=2m+1\\) the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+2y=2m+1,\\dots ,4m+1\\) are all sunny and already cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\nA complete verification consists in showing that for every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) either\n\\(a+2b\\) or \\(2a+b\\) belongs to the chosen interval of constants; the verification is a routine case analysis that uses the inequalities\n\\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\nBecause the present answer does not contain the full case‑by‑case verification, the sufficiency of (7) remains unproved here.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nI have proved that any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must satisfy the inequality (7).  \nThe constructions that would show that every integer \\(k\\) obeying (7) can actually be realised are sketched but not fully verified; therefore the problem is only partially solved. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
