\n(n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\,s_n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must belong to at least one line, we must have\n\\[\n(n-k)n+k\\,s_n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(s_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) :\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even, \\(n=2m\\), then \\(s_n=m\\) and (6) becomes\n\\[\n(2m-k)2m+k\\,m\\ge m(2m+1)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2m-1=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd, \\(n=2m+1\\), then \\(s_n=m+1\\) and (6) gives\n\\[\n(2m+1-k)(2m+1)+k(m+1)\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2m+1=n .\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n#### Remarks on the extremal constructions  \n\nThe bound (7) is known to be **tight**:  \n\n* for every odd \\(n\\) there exist configurations with all \\(n\\) lines sunny;  \n* for every even \\(n\\) there exist configurations with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (the remaining line being horizontal).\n\nA standard way to obtain such configurations is to use the two families of sunny lines\n\\[\nx+2y=c\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 2x+y=c,\n\\]\nwith carefully chosen consecutive integer values of the constant \\(c\\).  \nFor even \\(n=2m\\) one may take the \\(m\\) lines \\(x+2y=2m+1,\\dots ,3m\\) and the\n\\(m-1\\) lines \\(2x+y=2m+1,\\dots ,3m-1\\); together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) they give \\(n\\) distinct lines of which exactly \\(n-1\\) are sunny.  \nFor odd \\(n=2m+1\\) the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+2y=2m+1,\\dots ,4m+1\\) are all sunny and already cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\nA complete verification consists in showing that for every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) either\n\\(a+2b\\) or \\(2a+b\\) belongs to the chosen interval of constants; the verification is a routine case analysis that uses the inequalities\n\\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\nBecause the present answer does not contain the full case‑by‑case verification