                "content": "solution.**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI agree with the items raised in the bug report.  In particular  \n\n* Lemma 1 was proved incorrectly (the case \\(|\\Delta|=1\\) was handled with a false statement and the bound \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) was never established rigorously).  \n* The counting argument that leads to the necessary inequality (6) therefore collapses.  \n* The constructions that were claimed to realise the extremal values of \\(k\\) were not verified; no proof was given that every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on one of the proposed lines.  \n* Moreover the answer did not treat the intermediate values of \\(k\\) (the problem asks for **all** admissible \\(k\\)).  \n\nConsequently the previous solution is incomplete and invalid.\n\n**b. Method Sketch (complete solution)**  \n\nThe problem is to determine, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid\\exists\\;n\\text{ distinct lines } \\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\n\\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines and }\n\\forall (a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Rightarrow (a,b)\\in\\bigcup_{i=1}^n\\ell_i\\ \\}.\n\\]\n\nThe solution consists of three parts.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A sharp necessary bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n*Lemma 1 (maximal number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line).*\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)), then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le s_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in the primitive integer form  \n\n\\[\np x+q y=r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll integer points on \\(\\ell\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(x_t,y_t)=(x_0+tq,\\;y_0-tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is a particular integer solution of (2).  \nFor consecutive points the sum of the coordinates changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=(x_{t+1}+y_{t+1})-(x_t+y_t)=q-p .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) we have \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\).\n\n*Case A.* \\(|\\Delta|\\ge 2\\).  \nAll points of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) have their sums \\(x_t+y_t\\) lying in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\).  \nSince the sum changes by at least \\(2\\) each step, the number of admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Case B.* \\(|\\Delta|=1\\).  \nThen \\(\\Delta= \\pm1\\) and consequently \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) differ by exactly one: \\(q=p\\pm1\\).  \nBoth \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are non‑zero, so one of them is positive and the other negative.  \nAssume \\(p>0\\) and \\(q<0\\) (the opposite sign is symmetric).  \nFrom (3) we see that when \\(t\\) increases, the \\(x\\)-coordinate decreases by \\(|q|\\ge1\\) while the \\(y\\)-coordinate increases by \\(p\\ge1\\).\n\nBecause all points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(x\\ge1\\) and \\(y\\ge1\\), the forward progression (increasing \\(t\\)) can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{x_0-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le x_0\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints, and the backward progression (decreasing \\(t\\)) at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{y_0-1}{p}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le y_0 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAdding (6) and (7) gives at most \\(x_0+y_0\\) points.  \nSince \\((x_0,y_0)\\in S_n\\), we have \\(x_0+y_0\\le n+1\\); moreover the two progressions share the point \\((x_0,y_0)\\), so the total number of distinct points is at most  \n\n\\[\nx_0+y_0-1\\le n .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\), the bound \\(n\\) is weaker than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); a sharper estimate is obtained by ob