nstructions attaining the bound  \n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly the maximal number of sunny lines given by (10).  The construction works for both parities and automatically yields configurations for every smaller value of \\(k\\) (see Part 3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The two families  \n\nFor an integer \\(c\\) define the lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_c &: x+2y = c ,\\\\\nM_c &: 2x+y = c .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBoth families consist of sunny lines (their slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\), respectively).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Odd \\(n=2m+1\\) (maximal \\(k=n\\))\n\nTake the \\(m+1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\,2m+1},L_{\\,2m+2},\\dots ,L_{\\,3m+1}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nand the \\(m\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nM_{\\,2m+2},M_{\\,2m+3},\\dots ,M_{\\,3m+1}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAltogether we have \\((m+1)+m=2m+1=n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\); write \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1=2m+2\\)).  \n\n*If \\(a\\ge b\\)*, then \\(2a+b = a+s\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge b\\ge1\\), we have \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n2a+b = a+s \\in [\\,s+1,\\;2s-1\\,]\\subseteq[\\,2m+3,\\;3m+1\\,].\n\\]\n\n(The left‑most value occurs for \\(a=1\\) and the right‑most for \\(a=s-1\\).)  \nBecause the interval \\([2m+3,3m+1]\\) is exactly the set of constants appearing in (13), the point lies on the line \\(M_{\\,2a+b}\\).\n\n*If \\(a<b\\)*, then \\(a+2b = b+s\\).  \nNow \\(1\\le b\\le s-1\\), so  \n\n\\[\na+2b = b+s \\in [\\,s+1,\\;2s-1\\,]\\subseteq[\\,2m+2,\\;3m+1\\,].\n\\]\n\nThe interval \\([2m+2,3m+1]\\) is precisely the set of constants used in (12); hence the point lies on the line \\(L_{\\,a+2b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (12)–(13).  All \\(n\\) lines are sunny,